| Simplified CFD (one-time design check) | M | H (design) | M | H | H | None (offline) | M | L | NICE TO HAVE |
| Full/real-time CFD | H | H | L | H | H | H | H | L | NOT WORTH IT |
| Reinforcement learning control (autonomous) | H (unproven) | M | L (risk) | M | H | H | H | L | NOT WORTH IT (now) |
| Liquid-cooling integration | M (if dense) | L | M | H | M | H | H | L | NOT WORTH IT (now) |
| Economizer/free-cooling integration | H | L | H | H (HVAC) | M | H | M | M | FUTURE |
| Fault detection (equipment-level) | M | L | H | M | M | L | M | M | FUTURE |

*Table 2. Economic classification of each candidate improvement. **"**Mkt fit**"** = fit with low-to-mid-range operators specifically.*

# **6. The Practical 80/20 Architecture**

The architecture below captures most of the benefit of an advanced thermal-management system without hyperscale cost or complexity. It follows the pipeline: Sensors -> Data Fusion -> Thermal State -> Prediction -> Optimization -> Control -> Feedback.

| **Layer** | **Inputs** | **Outputs** | **Recommended method** | **Hardware** | **Compute** | **Why it****'****s worth it** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Sensors | Rack inlet/outlet temp, basic airflow/pressure, power (PDU), IT utilization, ambient temp/humidity | Raw time-series streams | ASHRAE-aligned placement + a small number of low-cost airflow proxies; reuse existing PowerPrune power/utilization feed | Existing temp sensors + a handful of added pressure/anemometer points + PDU taps | None (edge collection only) | Closes the two biggest real gaps (airflow, power/load) for a small hardware add-on, not a redesign |
| Data Fusion | All sensor streams, timestamps, site metadata | Aligned, cleaned, gap-filled feature set per rack/zone | Simple time-alignment + outlier rejection (no ML needed here) | None beyond sensors | Low (stream processing) | Garbage-in/garbage-out protection is cheap and prevents downstream model errors |
| Thermal State | Fused features | Per-zone thermal state estimate (current temperature field, load-adjusted) | Physics-lite heat-balance/RC model as the backbone, with spatial interpolation (e.g., Kriging) to populate the full grid from a smaller number of real sensors | None additional | Low | Physically grounded state estimate that will not hallucinate outside training data, unlike a pure black-box model; interpolation keeps the visual heatmap dense without needing a sensor at every cell |
| Prediction | Thermal state + short history + workload/power signal | 15-60 min hotspot probability with confidence band | Hybrid physics + lightweight spatiotemporal ML correction (ConvLSTM in place of plain LSTM, since the grid is a map, not a flat feature vector) with a small ensemble for uncertainty | None additional | Low-Medium (small model, not GPU-class) | Captures the accuracy gains hybrid, spatially-aware models show over plain LSTM while staying cheap to run; uncertainty band restores operator trust |
| Optimization | Predicted state + constraints (ASHRAE envelope, equipment limits) | Recommended setpoint/action (or job-deferral suggestion, coordinated with GridSync/PowerPrune) | Rule-based or lightweight MPC over a small action space (not RL) | None additional | Low-Medium | MPC-style optimization over a few actuators is proven to cut cooling energy meaningfully (14-23%+ in published studies) without RL's data/safety burden |
| Control | Recommended action | Executed or operator-approved actuation (fan speed, setpoint, job defer) | Supervised control (human-approve initially, autonomous later once trust is earned) | Actuator interface to CRAC/CRAH or BMS (Phase 2/3 hardware integration) | None additional | This is where the literature says the real savings and incident-prevention live - prediction without this step is cosmetic |
| Feedback | Outcome of executed actions vs. prediction | Model recalibration signal, savings-meter updates | Periodic fine-tuning of the ML correction layer; log actions for audit | None additional | Low (batch, not real-time) | Keeps the model honest per site and feeds VerdeGrid's existing Live Savings Meter with real, not simulated, results |

## **6.1 Physics + Data + Control, not primarily a prediction model**

Yes - ThermOS should be repositioned as a Physics + Data + Control system rather than a prediction model with a dashboard attached. The physics-lite core provides a floor of correctness and generalization that pure ML cannot; the data layer (ML correction, anomaly detection) captures what physics alone misses; and the control layer is what converts any of this into money saved or incidents avoided. Removing any one of the three either breaks (physics alone: too rigid, misses real anomalies; data alone: brittle, ungrounded, and unexplainable; control alone: nothing to control intelligently) or leaves value on the table (prediction with no control captures none of the literature's quantified savings).

# **7. Metrics That Should Actually Be Optimized**

PUE alone should not be the target. It has well-documented blind spots: it worsens when IT load and utilization drop (rewarding waste rather than efficiency), ignores IT-side performance and workload usefulness, is sensitive to climate and to measurement boundary choices, and provides no reliability signal at all. Global average PUE has been roughly flat (about 1.55-1.59) for several years, suggesting that chasing PUE further has diminishing returns for most facilities - including this market segment.

Recommended metric set for ThermOS/VerdeGrid, in priority order for a low-to-mid operator:

- Thermal safety margin and violation count (time spent outside ASHRAE recommended/allowable envelope) - a leading reliability indicator, not just an efficiency one.

- Hotspot frequency and time-to-detect/time-to-resolve - directly measures whether prediction+control is doing its job.

- Cooling energy per unit IT load (not raw PUE) - normalizes for utilization so it doesn't reward idle capacity.

- Fan/pump energy specifically - the actuator-level metric that closed-loop control directly affects.

- Reliability/SLA impact (downtime avoided, thermal-related incidents avoided) - ties thermal management to the outcome operators actually care about financially; unplanned cooling-related downtime has been estimated around $9,000/minute in industry outage-cost studies.

- PUE, reported alongside the above, not instead of them - useful for external benchmarking and trend-tracking within one facility, poor as a sole optimization target.

- WUE/CUE, where the facility uses water cooling or reports carbon - relevant given VerdeGrid already tracks water (CoolSense) and carbon (GridSync).

- kWh saved, cost recovered, CO2 avoided - VerdeGrid's existing Live Savings Meter framing, which should be fed by the metrics above rather than by PUE alone.

# **8. ThermOS V2 - Final Decision**

## **KEEP**

| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 8x8 real-time heatmap + inlet/outlet delta tracking | Already a legitimate, standards-adjacent visualization that beats spreadsheet/no-monitoring baseline for this segment | Basic visibility gap | Retains the core UX asset judges and users already respond to | Low (sunk) | Keep as-is |
| LSTM-based hotspot prediction, 15-20 min | Consistent with published DC temperature-prediction accuracy at short horizons | Reactive-only cooling | Maintains the predictive positioning that differentiates VerdeGrid from monitoring-only tools | Low (sunk) | Keep, upgrade per FIX below |
| Z-score anomaly/gradient detection | A legitimate, well-precedented statistical method, consistent across ThermOS and CoolSense | Undetected abnormal drift | Cheap, explainable anomaly flagging | Low (sunk) | Keep |
| Hybrid real-sensor + simulation deployment model | Enables low-cost, no-install demo/early deployment matching the target market's budget reality | High upfront deployment cost/complexity | Preserves the phased, low-risk rollout path | Low | Keep |

## **FIX**

| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Add uncertainty bands to predictions | Point estimates erode trust after any false alarm; nearly every serious deployment example in the literature treats this as a gap worth closing | Operator distrust of alerts | Higher perceived and actual reliability at near-zero added hardware cost | Low-Medium (software only) | MUST HAVE |
| Wire IT load/power telemetry (from PowerPrune) into the thermal model | Temperature-only models react to a lagging symptom; load-aware models have shown double-digit accuracy improvements in published comparisons | Poor prediction under sudden workload changes | Materially better forecasts, no new hardware | Low (integration only) | MUST HAVE |
| Move from pure ML to hybrid physics+ML core | Pure ML risks physically implausible predictions off-distribution and needs more data for the same accuracy | Fragile generalization across sites | More robust, more data-efficient, more explainable predictions | Medium | MUST HAVE |
| Upgrade plain LSTM to ConvLSTM; state clearly that the heatmap uses spatial interpolation | A flat LSTM discards the spatial structure an 8x8 grid was built to show; leaving sensor-vs-interpolated-cell ambiguous invites an easy challenge from a judge | Model ignores neighbor-to-neighbor heat spread; hardware claims read as overstated | Better spatial accuracy at no new hardware cost; a cleaner, more defensible technical story | Low-Medium (software only) | SHOULD HAVE |

## **ADD**

| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Basic airflow sensing (a handful of differential-pressure/anemometer points per aisle) | Temperature alone cannot separate an overloaded rack from a recirculation/bypass problem - the single largest sensing gap identified | Cannot diagnose root cause | Enables real diagnosis, not just detection | Low-Medium hardware | MUST HAVE |
| Supervised (human-approved) closed-loop control | Prediction alone captures little of the literature's quantified savings; hyperscale operators keep a human in the loop even at their scale | No action taken on predictions/detections | This is where the 9-23%+ energy savings and incident-prevention documented in the literature actually come from | Medium | MUST HAVE |
| Thermal zoning and adaptive setpoints | Cheap, high-leverage once airflow+power data exists | Coarse, one-size-fits-all cooling | Meaningful additional savings for modest added complexity | Low-Medium | SHOULD HAVE |
| Predictive maintenance for cooling equipment (fans, pumps) | Extends component life and avoids the kind of failure that turns a thermal alert into a real incident | Undetected mechanical degradation | Avoided emergency repairs and secondary thermal incidents | Medium | SHOULD HAVE |

## **REMOVE / AVOID (do not build now)**

| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Real-time/full CFD as a running product feature | Requires expert configuration and hours-to-days per run even for a small room; unusable online at any budget tier, including hyperscale | N/A - avoided complexity | Avoids wasted engineering effort on a tool nobody in this segment can run live | Would be very high if attempted | Do not build |
| Autonomous reinforcement-learning control | Even Google keeps a human able to intervene; unproven robustness under unseen conditions is a known open problem | N/A - avoided risk | Avoids safety and reliability risk disproportionate to this segment's budget and staffing | Would be very high if attempted | Not now |

## **MODIFY**

| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Positioning: from "prediction model with a dashboard" to "Physics + Data + Control system" | Matches where the literature says the value actually is, and is more defensible to technical judges | Undersells/mis-frames the roadmap's own Phase 3 control ambitions | Clearer, more credible narrative and a concrete engineering path to Phase 3 | Low (framing/roadmap only) | Do alongside FIX items |
| Terminology: replace "horizontal integration" with "cross-layer, hierarchical co-optimization" | "Horizontal integration" is a business-strategy term, not engineering usage; the alternative is both accurate and standard | Weak technical vocabulary under judge scrutiny | Stronger technical credibility with no engineering cost | None | Do now |

## **FUTURE (postpone deliberately)**

| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Liquid-cooling telemetry support | Only relevant once/if target customers adopt higher-density racks | N/A - not yet needed | Keeps ThermOS ready without spending now on a non-need | N/A | Future |
| Economizer/free-cooling control integration | A facility HVAC-design decision more than a software feature; valuable but outside ThermOS's current scope | N/A - facility-dependent | Preserves focus; revisit if VerdeGrid adds a facility-design advisory service | N/A | Future |
| Autonomous (non-supervised) control | Should only be attempted after a track record of safe, supervised MPC-driven action exists | N/A - sequencing decision | De-risks the eventual move to autonomy by earning it with data first | N/A | Future, after MPC track record |

# **9. Final Architecture and Strategic Conclusion**

## **9.1 Recommended pipeline**

Sensing (temp + airflow + power/load + ambient, ASHRAE-aligned placement)  ->  State Estimation (physics-lite heat-balance model, fused with sensor data)  ->  Prediction (hybrid physics+ML, multi-horizon, with uncertainty bands)  ->  Optimization (rule-based / lightweight MPC over setpoints, fan speed, and job-placement suggestions coordinated with PowerPrune and GridSync)  ->  Control (supervised actuation, human-approved initially)  ->  Feedback (recalibration + Live Savings Meter).

## **9.2 The smartest architecture for limited money, hardware, and compute**

Given real budget, hardware, and compute constraints, the smartest achievable architecture is a physics-lite thermal core (cheap, generalizable, physically grounded) corrected by a lightweight ML layer (captures what physics misses, stays small enough to run without GPU-class compute), fed by a small, targeted set of new sensors (airflow, reused power/load) rather than a dense CFD-grade grid, wrapped in a supervised control loop (captures the real savings the literature attributes to closed-loop action) with uncertainty-aware output (earns operator trust). This is deliberately less sophisticated than what Google, Schneider, or a national HPC lab can build - and that is the correct choice for this budget, not a compromise to apologize for.

## **9.3 Why not simply copy Schneider/Vertiv/hyperscale at a smaller scale**

Scaling down an enterprise or hyperscale architecture fails this market on three counts:

- Cost structure doesn't scale down linearly. Enterprise DCIM's $27-33/cabinet/month-plus-$50-200K-upfront pricing and 3-6 month deployments exist to amortize a platform built for arbitrary facility complexity; a smaller version of that same platform still carries most of that fixed integration and licensing cost relative to a much smaller facility's revenue.

- The sophistication that justifies hyperscale cost (CFD, digital twins, RL) is not the source of most of the quantified savings for this segment. The literature attributes most of the real, reproducible gains to closing the loop between prediction and action - something achievable with much simpler models than CFD or RL - and treats the widely-cited 40% neural-network figure as a claim that Google's own later, peer-reviewed work qualifies rather than repeats.

- A scaled-down hyperscale architecture inherits hyperscale assumptions (dedicated engineering staff, large training datasets, long commissioning windows, tolerance for months-long deployment) that simply do not hold for a low-to-mid operator.

**The intended competitive advantage is therefore not ****"****we have the most complex AI model.****"**** It is:**

*"**We integrate the right thermal, power, workload, and environmental information into one affordable system that captures most of the practical efficiency benefits of advanced data-center management without hyperscale-level complexity.**"*

That claim is now backed, item by item, by the evidence in Sections 1-8: the strategy is validated by the cross-layer/co-optimization literature, the competitive gap is mapped honestly against named vendors, the real limitations are diagnosed down to Problem-Cause-Consequence, every candidate improvement is priced against complexity and market fit rather than theoretical maximum performance, and the resulting V2 design is a defensible, buildable architecture rather than a wish list.

